# Identity guard — one extended-regex pattern per line, '#' comments allowed.
#
# Copy to ../.identity-guard (beside the checkout, NOT inside it) or to
# ~/.config/skills/identity-guard, and fill in the identifiers that belong to
# your deployment. This file is the example; the real one never enters the repo,
# because its contents are exactly what must not be published.
#
# What belongs in it, each class learned from something that actually shipped:
#
#   people and companies — a fresh install once greeted its owner by the name of
#   the company that wrote the skill, from a hardcoded string in a prompt.
#
#   machines and hosts — a file copied from a working box kept that box's home
#   directory and read a database path that did not exist for two days.
#
#   ids, mailboxes, addresses — a chat id or mailbox left in code routes a
#   stranger's install back to you.
#
# Include OTHER machines and personas in your estate too, not just your own: a
# second install's hostname and a demo company's name are somebody's private
# information, and naming one in a comment is how it reaches a third party.

# people / companies
[Ff]irstname [Ll]astname
\bYourCompany\b
yourcompany\.example

# machines / hosts
/home/youruser
\byour-hostname\b
\byour-vps-alias\b

# ids / mailboxes / addresses
\b1234567890\b
you@yourcompany\.example
\b203\.0\.113\.10\b
